---
title: Roast beef tenderloin with red wine sauce
servings: 4
yield: 4 to 6 servings
tags: [beef, christmas, holiday, winter]
time: 2 hours 40 minutes
source: Beef Tenderloin with Red Wine Sauce by Jennifer Segal, Once Upon a Chef, https://www.onceuponachef.com/recipes/roast-beef-tenderloin-wine-sauce.html
---

== Red wine sauce ==

Melt 5 tbsp of the @unsalted butter{8%tbsp}(divided) in a #medium saucepan{} over medium-low heat. Cook the @shallots{3/4%cup}(finely chopped, 2 to 3 large) ~{7-8%minutes}, stirring now and then, until soft and clear.

Add the @red wine{1.25%cup}, @beef broth{3%cup}, @thyme{6%sprigs}, @kosher salt{1/4%tsp}, @ground black pepper{1/8%tsp} and @sugar{1%tsp}. Bring to a boil, then cook over medium heat ~{30%minutes} until reduced by about half.

Meanwhile, mash the remaining 3 tbsp butter, soft but not melted, with the @all-purpose flour{2%tbsp} into a smooth paste.

Turn the heat to low and fish out the thyme. Whisk in the paste a tablespoon at a time and simmer a few minutes until thickened.

> The sauce keeps in the fridge up to 3 days. Make it ahead and the roast is the only job on the day.

== Beef ==

Leave the @beef tenderloin{2-3%lb}(center-cut) out at room temperature ~{1%hour}.

Heat the oven to 400°F (205°C) with a rack in the middle.

Season the beef all over with @kosher salt{}(1/2 tsp per pound) and @ground black pepper{}(1/4 tsp per pound).

Heat the @vegetable oil{2%tbsp} in an #ovenproof skillet{} over medium-high heat until almost smoking. Sear the beef, turning with #tongs{}, ~{10%minutes} until browned on every side but one.

Turn the unseared side down and put the skillet in the oven. Roast until the centre reads 120 to 125°F (49 to 52°C) for medium-rare, about ~{15%minutes}. Take it out at 115 to 120°F for rare or 130 to 135°F for medium.

Move the beef to a board and tent loosely with foil ~{10-15%minutes}. It climbs about 5 degrees as it rests.

Pour the fat off the skillet. Set it over the heat, add the @beef broth{1/4%cup} and boil, scraping up the browned bits. Tip this into the sauce and bring the sauce to a simmer.

Slice the beef 1/2 inch thick and pass the sauce at the table.
